          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"lafayette-la-princesse-de-cleves-125bbc0f4d0e\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"The husband directly avows his romantic love for his wife: “je vous aime comme ma maîtresse.” Its principal force is truth-conditional, presenting his love as a fact that explains his jealousy and suffering. Although marriage frames the exchange, these words do not themselves undertake or renew a commitment. The emotional intensity remains part of an avowal rather than independently evidenced expressive/reflexive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The marital and accusatory setting gives the avowal relational weight, but there is insufficient evidence that the core utterance itself enacts an undertaking.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"A husband directly addresses his wife during a jealous and anguished confrontation.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The speaker contrasts his own romantic love for his wife with her love for another man: “Vous êtes ma femme, je vous aime comme ma maîtresse, et je vous en vois aimer un autre.” The avowal grounds his claim to be profoundly unhappy and intensifies his accusation. The phrase “comme ma maîtresse” distinguishes passionate or erotic love from merely formal marital attachment. His distress and the later “s'écria-t-il” establish emotional delivery, but they do not show that “je vous aime” functions as a reflex-like discharge rather than an emphatic assertion.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Vous êtes ma femme, je vous aime comme ma maîtresse”\",\"supports\":\"The speaker explicitly presents his romantic love for the addressee as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“et je vous en vois aimer un autre”\",\"supports\":\"The contrast between his love and her love for another makes the avowal part of a reasoned jealous complaint rather than an independent expressive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Je suis plus malheureux que je ne l'ai cru, et je suis le plus malheureux de tous les hommes.”\",\"supports\":\"His avowed love functions as an explanation for his suffering and jealousy; the passage is emotionally intense, but intensity alone does not support E.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The passage identifies the addressee as “ma femme” immediately before the love avowal.\",\"supports\":\"Marriage supplies relational context, but the utterance does not explicitly promise, undertake, or renew an obligation, weighing against substantial P support.\",\"confidence\":0.94}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal. The surrounding jealousy, marital claim, and anguish are contextual features and do not require P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
